---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import TextFieldBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/TextFieldBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="text-field-user-invalid">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>:invalid</code> matches as soon as the page loads. A required field that
      is still empty turns red before anyone has typed a thing, and the form opens
      looking like it already failed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The usual fix is JavaScript that adds a <code>touched</code> or <code
        >dirty</code
      > class on blur and styles errors off that class. Not anymore.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:user-invalid</code> only matches after the user has changed the field
        and left it, or tried to submit the form
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:has(:user-invalid)</code> lifts that state to the wrapper, so the border,
        affixes and helper text all react
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the input covers errors the browser can't
        know about, like ones from the server
      </li>
      <li>
        Only private custom properties change, every rule that reads them
        follows
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.text-field {
  --accent: var(--primary);
  --border: var(--field-border-color);
  --helper: var(--field-helper-color);
  --label: var(--text-muted);
}

.text-field:focus-within .field {
  border-color: var(--accent);
}

.text-field:has([aria-invalid="true"], :user-invalid) {
  --accent: var(--invalid-color);
  --border: var(--invalid-color);
  --helper: var(--invalid-text-color);
  --label: var(--invalid-text-color);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <TextFieldBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:user-invalid</code> is Baseline since 2023, so this works in every
        current browser
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>[aria-invalid="true"]</code> and <code>:user-invalid</code> share one
        <code>:has()</code> list, so a browser that can't parse <code
          >:user-invalid</code
        > drops the whole rule and the field stays neutral
      </li>
      <li>
        Native validation still runs: the form won't submit and the browser
        shows its own message
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
